    "created_at": "2026-10-14T10:26:16Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-14T12:28:41Z",
    "body": "The reproduction here makes a useful execution-boundary regression: partial parsing can stay available for display, while token-limit termination must never authorize the repaired call's side effect. Complete JSON plus `finish_reason: \"length\"` is a separate useful negative case.\n\nI maintain [prefix-safe-json](https://github.com/canblmz1/prefix-safe-json). There is now a [standalone installed-package comparison](https://github.com/canblmz1/prefix-safe-json/tree/main/examples/langchain-consumer) using the report's exact `@langchain/openai@1.5.8` and `@langchain/core@1.2.9`. Real `ChatOpenAI.stream()` and PSJ receive the same original mock-SSE fixture. The SDK projects the cutoff as `{message:\"hel\"}` with no invalid call; caller dispatch through PSJ produces zero effects. Complete JSON plus token-limit termination also produces zero effects. CI checks the candidate tarball in a separate npm consumer.\n\nWould you be interested in trying that boundary in your own development project? This comparison does not provide a production LangChain adapter or exercise AgentExecutor/LangGraph; parsed `AIMessage.tool_calls` cannot recreate raw evidence. An [integration result or blocker](https://github.com/canblmz1/prefix-safe-json/issues/new?template=integration.yml) with your installed versions would help identify whether the boundary is practical. This is an optional comparison, separate from the local fix in #11494.",
